---
import {
  FileVideo,
  FolderOpen,
  Monitor,
  Smartphone,
  Sparkles,
} from "@lucide/astro";

const folderTree = `Media/                 <- Select this folder for Bulk import
  Inception (2010)/    <- One folder per movie
    Inception.mp4
  Breaking Bad/        <- Select this folder for Import folder / Single movie or series
    Bonus.mkv          <- Directly inside the title folder -> appears under "Default"
    Season 1/          <- Subfolder name appears in the season dropdown
      S01E01.mkv
      S01E02.mkv
    Season 2/
      S02E01.mkv`;
---

<section
  id="local-content"
  class="rounded-2xl border border-white/10 bg-vega-surface p-7 md:p-10"
>
  <div
    class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-full border border-white/15 bg-white/5 px-4 py-1.5 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-vega-accent"
  >
    <FolderOpen class="h-4 w-4" aria-hidden="true" />
    Local Content
  </div>

  <h1
    class="mt-4 font-headline text-[clamp(2rem,4vw,3.1rem)] font-black tracking-tight text-white"
  >
    Add and Format Local Content
  </h1>

  <p class="mt-3 max-w-[72ch] text-base leading-relaxed text-vega-muted">
    Play your own movies and series stored on your device inside Vega's Library,
    complete with season grouping, artwork, episode titles, and playback resume.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-6 space-y-5">
    <article class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-vega-surface-hi p-5">
      <h2 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold text-white">
        1. How to Add Local Content
      </h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-black/20 p-4">
          <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-white">
            <Smartphone class="h-4 w-4 text-vega-accent" aria-hidden="true" />
            <h3 class="font-headline text-base font-bold">
              Android &amp; Android TV
            </h3>
          </div>
          <ol
            class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-vega-muted marker:text-vega-accent"
          >
            <li>Open <strong class="text-white">Library</strong>.</li>
            <li>
              Tap the <strong class="text-white">Local content</strong> folder
              icon in the top bar.
            </li>
            <li>
              Choose <strong class="text-white">Import folder</strong> (one movie
              or series) or <strong class="text-white">Bulk import</strong> (a parent
              folder with multiple titles).
            </li>
            <li>
              Select your folder and allow persistent access when Android prompts.
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p class="mt-3 text-xs leading-relaxed text-vega-muted">
            Tip: Android blocks persistent access to top-level system roots such
            as internal storage root or the root Downloads folder. Create and
            select a subfolder such as <code
              class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white">Media</code
            >.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="rounded-lg border border-white/10 bg-black/20 p-4">
          <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-white">
            <Monitor class="h-4 w-4 text-vega-accent" aria-hidden="true" />
            <h3 class="font-headline text-base font-bold">
              Desktop (Windows, macOS, Linux)
            </h3>
          </div>
          <ol
            class="mt-3 list-decimal space-y-1.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-vega-muted marker:text-vega-accent"
          >
            <li>Open <strong class="text-white">Library</strong>.</li>
            <li>
              Click <strong class="text-white">Import folder</strong> in the header.
            </li>
            <li>
              Choose <strong class="text-white">Single movie or series</strong> or
              <strong class="text-white">Bulk import</strong>.
            </li>
            <li>Select your folder, review each title, and click Import.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-vega-surface-hi p-5">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-white">
        <FolderOpen class="h-5 w-5 text-vega-accent" aria-hidden="true" />
        <h2 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold">
          2. Recommended Folder Structure
        </h2>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-vega-muted">
        Organizing each movie or show into its own folder lets Vega group
        seasons cleanly and pick up title names automatically.
      </p>
      <div
        class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-black/30 p-4"
      >
        <pre
          class="m-0 font-mono text-[13px] leading-6 text-white/90"><code>{folderTree}</code></pre>
      </div>
      <ul
        class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-vega-muted marker:text-vega-accent"
      >
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Bulk import:</strong> Scans each immediate subfolder
          as its own movie or series. Any loose video files placed directly inside
          the parent folder are grouped together under that parent folder's name.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Seasons &amp; subfolders:</strong> Subfolder
          names appear in the season dropdown and sort naturally (for example,
          <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Season 2</code
          > before <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Season 10</code
          >). Nested subfolders such as <code
            class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Season 1/Specials</code
          > are included too, while files directly inside a title folder appear under
          <strong class="text-white">Default</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Supported video extensions:</strong>
          <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white">.mkv</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.mp4</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.m4v</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.avi</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.mov</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.webm</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.ts</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.m2ts</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.mpeg</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.mpg</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.3gp</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.wmv</code
          >, and <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >.flv</code
          >.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-vega-surface-hi p-5">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-white">
        <FileVideo class="h-5 w-5 text-vega-accent" aria-hidden="true" />
        <h2 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold">
          3. Formatting Filenames for Episode &amp; Metadata Matching
        </h2>
      </div>
      <ul
        class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-vega-muted marker:text-vega-accent"
      >
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white"
            >Explicit season + episode (recommended, works in any folder):</strong
          >
          Include <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >S01E01</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >s01e01</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >S1 E01</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >S01.E01</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >S01_E01</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >S01-E01</code
          >, or <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >1x01</code
          > anywhere in the filename (for example <code
            class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Show.Name.S01E01.1080p.mkv</code
          > or <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Show 1x02.mp4</code
          >).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Season folder + episode number:</strong> Place
          files inside a folder named <code
            class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white">Season 1</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Season 02</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Season_1</code
          >, or <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >S1</code
          > and name files with <code
            class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white">Episode 1</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Ep 02</code
          >, <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white">E03</code
          >, or a leading episode number like <code
            class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >01 - Pilot.mkv</code
          > or <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >02.mp4</code
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Automatic folder name cleanup:</strong> When
          pre-filling metadata search, Vega strips dots, underscores,
          <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >[bracketed tags]</code
          >, and release years — for example, <code
            class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Breaking.Bad.2008.[1080p]</code
          > becomes <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >Breaking Bad</code
          >.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Unmatched files still work:</strong> Files that
          do not match a season/episode pattern still display and play normally using
          their original filenames.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>

    <article class="rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-vega-surface-hi p-5">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 text-white">
        <Sparkles class="h-5 w-5 text-vega-accent" aria-hidden="true" />
        <h2 class="font-headline text-xl font-bold">
          4. Matching Metadata, Display Names &amp; Rescanning Later
        </h2>
      </div>
      <ul
        class="mt-3 list-disc space-y-2.5 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-vega-muted marker:text-vega-accent"
      >
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Choose movie or series:</strong> During import
          review — or anytime later via <strong class="text-white"
            >Edit metadata</strong
          > on the title's info page — search by title or switch to <strong
            class="text-white">IMDb ID</strong
          > (for example <code class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >tt0944947</code
          >) and pick <strong class="text-white">Movie</strong> or <strong
            class="text-white">Series</strong
          >. Matching is optional; select <strong class="text-white"
            >Use folder name instead</strong
          > (Android) or <strong class="text-white">Use folder name</strong> (Desktop)
          to keep local folder and file names without artwork.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Metadata names toggle:</strong> When enabled,
          matched movies label the playable item as <code
            class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white">Movie</code
          > and matched episodes show as <code
            class="rounded bg-white/10 px-1.5 py-0.5 text-white"
            >S1 E1 · Episode Title</code
          > along with thumbnails and episode summaries. Turn this off if you prefer
          to always see original filenames.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Save to library:</strong> Assign imported titles
          to one or more Library categories, or leave the default selection so they
          appear under <strong class="text-white">All</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong class="text-white">Updating files later:</strong> When you add
          or remove video files in an already-imported folder, pull down to refresh
          on the title page on Android, or click <strong class="text-white"
            >Rescan folder</strong
          > on Desktop.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</section>
